This week, the main discussion was about which audio will be used in the project: 
the poem or the song. We’re still talking with Nadia about it, since the choice affects how the colours will be mapped to sound.
A song has a melody and rhythm that could be broken into notes or sections, while a poem is spoken voice,
so it would probably work better as separate recorded clips triggered by each colour. Either way, the decision should come from Nadia,
because the audio is personal and connected to the meaning of the piece.

Waiting on the ceramic piece

We’re also trying to stay updated on the ceramic piece Nadia is going to give us. 
We need to know whether it will be ready in time for us to do colour testing on it and connect it to the music or poem. 
So far we’ve only tested the sensor on screen colours, so testing on the real ceramic is an important next step.

If the piece isn’t ready in time, my backup plan is to make a piece myself, inspired by Nadia’s work. It wouldn’t replace hers, 
but it would let us show a working representation of what the final installation could do. It would also give us a surface to test on, 
so we can see how the sensor reacts to glazed, uneven ceramic.

Scanner redesign

The scanner casing is still being reworked so that it’s more of a cylinder shape. A cylinder should be more comfortable to hold,
and it’s simpler and more consistent than the ridged form we tested last time.

We also received Arduino Nano audio players,
which is a big change for the project. The new version of the scanner will be wireless. 
Instead of being connected to a laptop by USB, the scanner will need to detect the colour and play the sound on its own.
This creates a lot of new work:

Coding: rewriting the code so the colour detection triggers audio directly on the Nano instead of sending data to a computer
Design: making the casing fit the Nano, the audio module, the sensor, and a power source, all inside a smaller cylinder shape
Functionality: figuring out power, how the sound will be heard (speaker placement and volume),
and making sure the sensor still sits at the right distance from the bead
Reflection

Going wireless feels like the right direction,
because it makes the scanner feel more like a finished object and less like a tech demo plugged into a laptop.
Visitors could hold it and move it across the tapestry freely, which fits better with the idea of the ceramic “remembering” a voice. 
At the same time, it adds a lot of uncertainty with the final prototype deadline coming up,
so we’ll need to test each part step by step instead of putting everything together at the end.

